  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MDRI-507831-research</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This research contributes to Oxfam in Vietnam&apos;s research and influencing on the Human Economy in Vietnam. Conceptualized as an alternative way of thinking about inclusive and sustainable development, the Human Economy addresses the paradox in social -economic-environmental nexus through systemic transformations in public services, progressive tax, inclusive business, gender equality and citizens participation.

Sustained socioeconomic development successes,  current &#x201c;green growth&#x201d; commitments and programmes,  and the active participation of the media towards the agenda, are some of the indications of Vietnam&#x2019;s aspirations towards human-centred development. Preliminary literature review indicated that voices of non state actors  - despite their significant contributions to Vietnam&#x2019;s development in the past and future - are often less influential in the state-led development agenda. Rather than being a top-down,technocratic or externally imposed, there are examples of social movements and bottom-up, small-scale initiatives that suggest thatthe idea of a more human-centered development model has internally emerged and interplay with the grand development narrative imposed by the state. A more comprehensive understanding of (divergent) perceptions, agendas, interest and resources of the human-centered development model(s) will critically help Oxfam in Vietnam continue its influencing works on the Human Economy model with the Vietnam government, social organisations, businesses and the public.

With these in mind, the selected partner - Mekong Development Research Institute/MDRI - has been selected for the project with their research expertise in development economics and inequality. MDRI has been Oxfam&apos;s research partners in several flagship projects on inequality in Vietnam. MDRI will base on Oxfam&apos;s TOR to design the research, manage their consultant researchers, produce the research report, conduct a consultative workshop with relevant stakeholders, and submit final reports of the research projects in Vietnamese and English languages.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="80">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mekong Development Research Institu</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mekong Development Research Institute (MDRI) is an independent scientific research agency. MDRI believes that research plays an indispensable role in social change and development.
The key services focus on Poverty Reduction, Ethnic Minority, Agriculture and Rural Development, Labor and Immigration, Water and Sanitation, Education, Environment and Climate Change, Sector &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Market Research and Trade, and Governance. Through our research and consulting work, MDRI researchers have brought forward meaningfuland valuable research products, some of which have been published in internationally renowned journals including American Economic Journal, WorldDevelopment, and Health Economic Journal. Others were available under forms of consulting reports, policy briefs used for the Government leaders and presented in national and international conferences

MDRI strives to become a leading research institute in policyand development in the Mekong region. MDRI hopes that their high quality research will make significant and positive contributions towards regional development as well as highligh challenges in opening Vietnam#s economy to share with countries with similar socialeconomic conditions.

MDRI is ranked No.1 among economic institutions in Vietnam and among the top 17% of 12,700 economic institutes in the world by IDEAS: Economics and Finance Research.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
